Name the ops a model ran, so each can be optimized where it lives. The model is {{ model }},
run by {{ framework }}; its config is config.json here. ops.json lists the ops that took
{{ coverage }} of device time, keyed by op: the framework op, the op that owns it, its share
and calls, every kernel it launched with their device time, its arguments (name, shape and
dtype, or value), and its call site. The kernel repositories on this machine are in
{{ repos }}.

For each key, find where the op is implemented -- the registration of its namespace, the
kernels it launches, the source behind them -- by reading the framework and the
repositories, not by guessing from names. Then write names.json:

{"ops": {"<key>": {
   "route": one of {{ routes | join(", ") }}:
       kernel:  a kernel in one of the repositories that Xe-Forge can rewrite;
       library: the op's time is a library primitive -- what is tuned is the call, in the
                repository that makes it;
       aten:    implemented inside PyTorch, with no repository here to edit;
       unnamed: none of these could be established -- say why in "via".
   "name": the symbol to grep for in the repository (the kernel, or the function making the
           library call); "repo": the repository's directory name under {{ repos }};
   "dsl": one of {{ dsls | join(", ") }} -- the language the kernel is written in, or for a
          library call the language the call is made in;
   "library": for a library call, which library (e.g. "onednn", "onemkl");
   "via": one line: how you established it (file:line);
   "family": one of {{ families | join(", ") }};
   "paged": true when an argument is a paged or slot-indexed pool (a KV cache, a block or
            page table, an SSM or conv state pool) whose touched part depends on the
            context, not its shape;
   "args_complete": false when the kernels read state the arguments do not show (weights,
            caches or states fetched by a layer name);
   "token_arg", "token_axis": the argument and axis that hold the tokens, when that is not
            the first tensor's first axis;
   "kernels": only when the op's kernels live in different places (a Python op launching
            several kernels): {"<kernel symbol substring>": {route, name, repo, dsl, via,
            family}} -- each group of kernels is then measured and optimized on its own;
   "gemm": only for a matrix multiply -- which argument is what, from the kernel's source;
           a list when the op multiplies by more than one weight:
     {"activation": "<arg>", "weight": "<arg>",
      "act_per_stored": <logical K elements per stored activation element, 1 unless packed>,
      "n_axis": <index of N in the weight's shape>,
      "weight_side": ["<arg>", ...]   every tensor read with the weight: weight, scales,
                                      zero points, bias -- not activation-side scales,
      "output": "<arg>" or null,
      "grouped": true for one call over several experts' matrices,
      "experts": "<integer argument holding this call's (local) expert count>" or null,
      "rows": one of {{ rows | join(", ") }} -- whether the activation's rows are tokens or
              token x expert rows}
 }},
 "model": {"topk": <experts each token is routed to, or null>,
           "experts": <routed experts in the model, or null>}}

Use the keys exactly as ops.json gives them, and argument names exactly as it lists them.
Before writing, check each name greps in its repository and each argument you name is one
the op has. An op you cannot place is "unnamed" with the reason, never a guess.
